1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,213
Clerks come down with slates and polite shoes. They have been told to make the unknown legible. They begin with whatever can be numbered.

2
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:13,617
They count grain, beds, lamps, and the height of the river. Nima, who keeps the river, already knows the height. She lets them measure, and she does not correct them in public.

3
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:13,617
A storm on the surface cuts the camp and fills the infirmary tent. The stair fills with people who are bleeding through dust. They are brought the rest of the way down.

4
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:09,362
Vale calls the open hospital a trust campaign. Pema, wrapping a stranger's arm, does not look up. She says it is Tuesday.

5
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:10,213
More beds appear in the hospital by night. No one above ordered them. No one below writes a request. The wood simply gets used.

6
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:11,064
A memo praises the house for calming the camp. The white wall does not mention calm. It mentions thirst, weapons, and the direction of the stair.

7
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:09,787
The inventory has no line for names, songs, or the chair with the cushion. Imani adds a margin note: uncounted, and not empty.

8
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:11,915
Two books sit on one table. Adler's ledger records stock. Asha's lamp book records who lit which stair, and when. Only one of them is concerned with morning.

9
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:10,638
Imani stops sleeping in the camp. She says the instruments hear more clearly from below. This is true, and it is not the whole reason.

10
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:14,468
At the end of the count, the ledger has a new column, written by hand. It asks who the room is for. Most of Adler's staff leave the column blank. The house does not.
